module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Operators

let content(): String =
  """# Operators

## Comparison
  == != < > <= >=
  `==` and `!=` are structural on every type and are not traits, so no type can
  change what they mean. `< > <= >=` are `Stdlib.Compare`: every numeric type
  implements it, and so can yours. `docs traits` has how.

## Boolean
  && ||   and unary !x
  `!x` is Stdlib.Bool.not; `!x && y` is `(!x) && y`.

## Unwrap
  value?   f(x)?   (value |> transform)?
  Extract Ok/Some, or return Error/None from the nearest function/lambda.
  Success must still be wrapped in Ok/Some; error types must agree.
  No space before the ?. It applies to whatever comes right before it, so
  `a + f(x)?` unwraps just `f(x)`. To unwrap the sum, write `(a + b)?`.

## Numeric (all int types, Float, Float32, Decimal)
  + - * / % **   and unary -x
  Each is a trait: `+` is `Stdlib.Add`, then Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Modulo, Power, and `-x`
  is `Negate`. Both operands must be the same type: `1 + 2`, `1.0 + 2.0` and
  `1s8 * 2s8` all work; `1 + 2.0` is "Cannot perform numeric operation on Int64
  and Float". `"a" + "b"` is `"ab"`, since String implements Add.
  A type of your own joins with `impl Add for Vec = ...`.
  `/` is integer division on ints, float division on floats.
  `%` works on the integers and Decimal (the modulus must be > 0). Float and Float32 have no
  Modulo implementation.
  `**` (power) works on every integer type, Float and Float32, and nests right:
  `2 ** 3 ** 2` is `2 ** (3 ** 2)`.
  Unary `-x` negates signed ints, Float and Float32; an unsigned type has no Negate
  implementation, and `-x` on one says so.
  A fixed-width integer WRAPS on overflow, it does not raise: `9223372036854775807 + 1`
  is `-9223372036854775808`, and `-x` on a type's minimum is that minimum again.
  `BigInt` (`1z`) is arbitrary-precision and does not wrap. Dividing by zero raises.
  Float arithmetic follows IEEE (overflow -> infinity).
  Decimal (`19.99dec`) is exact base-10 with up to 28 places: `+ - * / %`, unary `-` and
  comparisons. No `**`; `Stdlib.Decimal.power(d, n)` takes a whole Int64 power. A result
  past 96 bits raises; `/` gives as many places as fit, so follow it with
  `Stdlib.Decimal.round(q, 2)` (or `roundWith` and a `RoundingMode`) to fix them; both
  return a Result, an Error for places outside 0 to 28. The places written are kept
  (`1.10dec` prints 1.10) but `1.10dec == 1.1dec`.

## Bitwise (every integer type; Float has no implementation)
  & | ^ << >>   and unary ~x
  `&` and, `|` or, `^` xor, `<<`/`>>` shift, `~x` NOT (two's complement).
  Traits like the rest: `&` is `BitwiseAnd`, `|` `BitwiseOr`, `^` `BitwiseXor`, `~` `BitwiseNot`,
  `<<` `ShiftLeft`, `>>` `ShiftRight`, so your own type can implement them.
  Both operands must be the same integer type, including the shift count:
  `1 << 4`, not `1 << 4s8`.
  Fixed-width results wrap, and so does the shift count: it is taken modulo the bit
  width, as in Rust and C#. `1u8 << 9u8` is `2u8` (a shift by 1, not 0), and
  `1s8 << -1s8` shifts by 7. `>>` on a signed type keeps the sign.
  `BigInt` (`1z`) is arbitrary-precision, so a shift count that is negative or too
  large to be an Int32 raises instead.

  `|` is also the match-arm separator, and inside a `match` the arm wins.
  Parenthesize to mean bitwise-or there:
    match x with
    | A -> (a | b)

## String
  +    concat

## List
  No operator. Spread in a list literal instead:
  [x, ...xs]      put x in front
  [...a, ...b]    concat, lowers to Stdlib.List.concat

## Pipe
  value |> fn
  (if c then a else b) |> fn   // an if or match needs parens

## Precedence
  Loosest to tightest. `**` nests right; everything else groups left.
    ||
    &&
    == != < > <= >=
    |
    ^
    &
    << >>
    + -
    * / %
    **
    f(a, b)         (call)
    x.field  x?     (postfix, tightest)
  Note the bitwise levels bind TIGHTER than the comparisons, as in Python:
  `a & b == c` is `(a & b) == c`."""
